WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:07.260
These examples show how tests and
inference, and relatively simple

2
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.620
tests for comparing means,
have become essential tools

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.000
in all the so-called experimental
sciences, allowing us to test for

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.860
significant differences, typically
between groups, in variables of interest.

5
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.040
Statisticians have been developing
all these statistical methods for

6
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.740
over 100 years. But they have had

7
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.300
computers to calculate these statistics
for 30 to 40 years, and quite quickly,

8
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.880
they realized that it might
be useful to go beyond

9
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.700
calculating a mean, for
example, just once, and instead

10
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.940
potentially recalculate it dozens,
hundreds, or even thousands of

11
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:58.700
times, using our original sample, which is
meant to represent the parent population.

12
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.060
So, here is a small example
where we randomly generated

13
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:09.260
heights from 158 to 184 cm,
forming our original sample.

14
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.980
But rather than simply
calculating the mean of x once,

15
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:20.180
we will resample and calculate

16
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:26.920
the mean 2,000 times by sampling
from these original values,

17
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.520
taking a random sample. We will
draw a new sample of 10 values

18
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.180
selected randomly from the values
available in the first sample.

19
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.480
We therefore calculate 2,000
means instead of just one. We can

20
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:49.420
display them in a histogram and calculate
the quantiles of the distribution

21
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.200
of these 2,000 calculated
means. So, to begin with,

22
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.760
we need the original sample
mean. We can compare it with the

23
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.100
mean of the 2,000 samples obtained, and
also examine the confidence interval

24
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.900
around the location of the mean
calculated from this sample.

25
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.580
This is another way of presenting these
so-called bootstrap methods, which

26
00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:21.680
are particularly useful and
interesting when our sample is

27
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.760
small, because when a sample
contains many observations,

28
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.740
ultimately, the variable, the
mean we calculate or the test

29
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.760
we perform on these observations
is already very reliable.

30
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:40.280
The number of observations
in the sample improves the

31
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:45.220
quality of the inference we
can make from that sample.

32
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.620
So, it is when samples are
small that it becomes useful

33
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.380
to use bootstrapping to get a
better idea of the distribution

34
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.540
of our statistic of interest:
a mean, a t-test, or a p-value.

35
00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:02.900
Whatever statistic we are interested
in, we can recalculate it dozens,

36
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.840
hundreds, or even thousands
of times and get an idea of

37
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.360
how our results vary around the initial value.
